module Core

import Native

{-
   ==========================================================================================
   A run of values in a format hole - the separator, and the brackets a debug print wants.
   ==========================================================================================

   `Core/Join.yana`'s `joinWith` is the fold: it builds the answer with `++`, so it needs `Monoid` and
   `Append` on the *elements* and it produces a `String` that the format hole it was written for then
   copies. Neither is what a hole wants. A hole already has a sink and a measured size, so what it
   wants is something that writes into one - and what it needs of an element is `Show` and nothing
   else, because showing is the only thing it does to one.

   So this is the formatter beside that fold, exactly as `hexBytes` is the formatter beside `hexOf`:
   `print("{joined(names, \", \")}")` writes the elements and the separators straight into the
   caller's buffer with no intermediate anywhere, where `joinWith` allocates one string per fold and
   one more per `++`. `joinWith` stays, because a program that wants the joined text as a *value* is
   asking for a different thing.

   **A record of its own rather than a style and a `Formatted`, and `Field.yana` states the rule.**
   A style is a recipe for rendering a *leaf* and is a record of scalars, which a separator is not -
   it is a string, so a style holding one would make every `Formatted` over it a root of a reference
   (`Core/Format.yana`, on why provenance is field-insensitive). And like a column, this describes
   what to do with renderings somebody else made, so it works on anything showable and has no reader:
   a separator is not recoverable from the text unless it cannot appear inside an element, which is a
   promise this cannot make.
-}

{-
   The elements and what goes between them.

   Two borrows in one record, which is what this needed that no other formatter here did: the
   elements are a window into the caller's container and the separator is a view of the caller's
   string, and neither may outlive the expression that built it. The return-root check bounds both,
   so a `Joined` is a thing to hand to a format hole rather than a thing to keep - which is the same
   sentence `Format/Number.yana` writes about its own subject, with one more root in it.

   `'[a]` in the field and `[a]` on the parameter are the same window in the two positions' own
   spellings, exactly as `HexFormat`'s are: `[T]` in a field is the *owner*, so a field that is a view
   says so with the tick.
-}
pub data Joined(a) {
    value: '[a],
    separator: 'String
}

pub fn (Show(a)) joined(values: '[a], separator: 'String) -> Joined(a) =
    Joined {value: values, separator: separator}

instance (Show(a)) Show(Joined(a)):
    fn show(value: Joined(a), &to: String):
        let items = value.value
        let count = length(items)
        let &i = 0 :: Size

        while i < count:
            if i > 0 then String.push(to, value.separator)
            show(items[i], to)
            i = i + 1

    {-
       The elements' own bounds, summed, plus the separators - and `Nothing` as soon as one element
       cannot say.

       A loop in a `showBound` rather than a constant, which is what a container makes unavoidable:
       the count is a runtime value, so even an element whose bound is a literal contributes a
       multiplication. What it buys is that a run of a thousand `Int`s sizes its buffer once and
       appends into it, where an unbounded answer would grow it a handful of times.

       `Nothing` propagates rather than being replaced by a partial sum, because a bound may not be
       short and a partial one would be.
    -}
    fn showBound(value: Joined(a)) -> Maybe(Size):
        let items = value.value
        let count = length(items)
        if count <= 0 then return Just(0)

        let &total = length(value.separator) * (count - 1)
        for i in 0 .. count:
            let Just(units) = showBound(items[i]) | return Nothing
            total = total + units

        return Just(total)

{-
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   And the brackets, which is what a container shows as when nothing says otherwise
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

   `checkEqual(xs, ys)` printing two containers as `[1, 2, 3]` is the whole reason these exist: an
   assertion that fails is a debug print, and a container with no `Show` instance is one that cannot
   be named in the diagnostic that failed on it.

   **The spelling is the source's own** - `[`, the elements, `, ` between them, `]` - because that is
   the one rendering a reader does not have to be taught and because a value printed this way can be
   pasted back into a program. `[]` for an empty one, which is what the language writes too.

   **Elements go through `show` and not through a quoting form**, and that is a real limitation
   stated rather than hidden: `["a", "b"]` prints as `[a, b]`, since `Show(String)` writes a string as
   itself and there is no second class here saying "write yourself as source". A caller who wants the
   quoted spelling has it in one expression - `joined(map(xs, quoted), ", ")` around brackets of its
   own - and the day a `Source` class is worth its weight, these two instances are where it lands.

   **One instance, over the container**, and its members take the *window* because that is what
   writing `value: [a]` in them asks for. An owner reaches it by converting its argument, which is
   what every `[a]` parameter does; a window reaches it because a window and the container it is
   over are one logical type. There used to be a
   second instance here, over `Array(a)`, whose whole body was `show(elements(value), to)` - the
   conversion written out by hand because instance selection would not do it.
-}
instance (Show(a)) Show([a]):
    fn show(value: [a], &to: String):
        String.pushUnit(to, "[")
        show(Joined {value: value, separator: ", "} :: Joined(a), to)
        String.pushUnit(to, "]")

    fn showBound(value: [a]) -> Maybe(Size) =
        match showBound(Joined {value: value, separator: ", "} :: Joined(a)):
            Just(units) -> Just(units + 2)
            Nothing -> Nothing
